WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.920
Renee Dixon lives in Indianapolis,

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.750
and has worked in early childhood education for 25 years.

3
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.180
And today she works as a preschool director.

4
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.890
But what makes her so special

5
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.340
is that she has spent every weekend

6
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.210
for the last four years driving for Uber

7
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:26.210
in order to buy supplies, gifts, clothes for her students.

8
00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.970
I'm so excited to talk to her.

9
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.479
Renee, are you there?

10
00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:35.255
I am, how are you?

11
00:00:35.255 --> 00:00:38.005
(audience claps)

12
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.800
It's so nice to meet you.

13
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.390
I have been so looking forward to meeting you.

14
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.980
So how did you first become passionate about education?

15
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.960
Like was there a moment in your life?

16
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.160
Was it over time?

17
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.230
What was it?

18
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.890
It was, I'm from Mississippi, so I used to work

19
00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.540 line:15%
at the Mississippi State Penitentiary men death row.

20
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.370 line:15%
And I start seeing younger

21
00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.430 line:15%
and younger people come into the system.

22
00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.190 line:15%
And I said, something's wrong.

23
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.380 line:15%
Somewhere someone missed something.

24
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.510
So when I moved to Indianapolis, I said,

25
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.080
I need to be there, I have to be there.

26
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.860
This is my calling, to be there for kids.

27
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.160
And you think that's maybe like the missing link,

28
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.340
as far as if there is love, consistency,

29
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.340
availability, resources, that that will have

30
00:01:35.642 --> 00:01:38.050
a trickle-down effect or matriculate,

31
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.370
as they say in your world, into young adults?

32
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.920
Yes, I mean, in the first five years

33
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.210
of a child's life is the most important five years.

34
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.870
And they learn so much,

35
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.850 line:15%
their little brains are like sponges.

36
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.610 line:15%
And every little thing that you do,

37
00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.610 line:15%
and every little science experiment

38
00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.640 line:15%
that you do with them, it's amazing.

39
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.284
Even just put a vinegar with baking soda,

40
00:02:02.284 --> 00:02:05.420 line:15%
they're like, oh my God, how did you do that?

41
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.410 line:15%
Is that magic? (laughs)

42
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.870 line:15%
That's literally my daughter in a nutshell.

43
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.260
Every day, all around the house.

44
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.099
I call her the alchemist anarchist.

45
00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:16.940
(audience laughs)

46
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.540
What gave you the idea, then, to start driving

47
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.490
for Uber on weekends to raise money?

48
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.880
Well, I worked for a company.

49
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.140
They didn't really have a budget for the classroom.

50
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.440
And my students needed everything.

51
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.440
And I said, I have to be there, I have to be that person.

52
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.490
So at least taking some of the load off the parents.

53
00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.410
So I started doing that.

54
00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.770
And then Christmas came, and I bought Christmas presents.

55
00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.770
But this last year, it was harder because COVID.

56
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.989
And a lot of my families who are living

57
00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:56.990
below the poverty line,

58
00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:01.320
they told me they can't afford presents for their kids.

59
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.996 line:15%
And I'm like, no child should ever wake up

60
00:03:03.996 --> 00:03:05.540 line:15%
not have a Christmas present,

61
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.580 line:15%
because I've been there before.

62
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.340
Growing up, my mom was a single mom.

63
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.370
And I said, no, your child's gonna have

64
00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:14.940
a Christmas and a Christmas present.

65
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.670
And I said, and then if you have children,

66
00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.070
other children, they are gonna have it.

67
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:25.070
So I have 50 students and 20 siblings, so 70 kids.

68
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.340
I mean, it was a lot of work.

69
00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.750
But if I didn't do it, then who else?

70
00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.730
Because a lot of parents were used

71
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.100
to being that giving person.

72
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.060
And now they have to be the person that received the help.

73
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.210
And they were kind of embarrassed about it.

74
00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.980
I'm like, there's nothing to be embarrassed about.

75
00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.723
Everybody's gonna go through something once in their life.

76
00:03:47.723 --> 00:03:49.960
I'm here, I got it.

77
00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.619
I told them I got it, I got it.

78
00:03:51.619 --> 00:03:52.891
I'ma do it, I'm gonna do it.

79
00:03:52.891 --> 00:03:55.450 line:15%
There are nights I'm like, you ain't gonna do it.

80
00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.730 line:15%
I'm like, I'm gonna do it, I'm gonna do it, I'm gonna do it.

81
00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:02.060 line:15%
I mean, when I work seven to six Monday through Fridays.

82
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.160 line:15%
And on Fridays, when I get off at six o'clock,

83
00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:10.160 line:15%
I gas my car up, work until one, two o'clock in the morning,

84
00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.340
take a little nap, get up at eight, sometimes nine,

85
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.548
drive till one, two o'clock Sunday morning,

86
00:04:18.548 --> 00:04:22.800
take a little nap, get up eight, nine o'clock,

87
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.040
and drive till four o'clock on Sundays.

88
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.100
And then after the four, I sleep. (laughs)

89
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.130
I have so many thoughts and feelings right now.

90
00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.450
A, I wanna be your best friend.

91
00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.497
B, I want everyone to be like you.

92
00:04:34.497 --> 00:04:38.710
C, I'm just so glad that you're in the world,

93
00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.840
that you grew up with a single mom,

94
00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.330
that you worked on death row.

95
00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.690
You saw a discrepancy, you went

96
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.195
and you studied and got your degree in psychology

97
00:04:49.195 --> 00:04:52.740
and early child education, became a teacher.

98
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.710
Take care of the students, provide them with shoes,

99
00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:59.490
clothes, shelter, like love, a good attitude,

100
00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.280
and then go and help their parents

101
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.300
and drive all weekend to fund it.

102
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.300
I just had to do a recap because it's just so incredible.

103
00:05:09.712 --> 00:05:10.545
(audience claps)

104
00:05:10.545 --> 00:05:11.378
I mean, it's something that I have to do.

105
00:05:11.378 --> 00:05:12.495
(audience cheers)

106
00:05:12.495 --> 00:05:14.365
Oh, thank you.

107
00:05:14.365 --> 00:05:15.550
It's something I have to do.

108
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.770
I just wanna say, I thank you for going above and beyond.

109
00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:23.770
And BP wants to give you this $5,000 gift card

110
00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.656
that can be used at BP and AMOCO stations.

111
00:05:27.656 --> 00:05:28.570
(audience claps)

112
00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.500
And then we wanted to get something else for your kids.

113
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.750
So today Kidpik, the number one clothing subscription box

114
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.240
for kids, wants to donate another $5,000 worth

115
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.013
of winter clothing for you and your students.

116
00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.546
I haven't done anything special.

117
00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:48.283
(audience claps)

118
00:05:48.283 --> 00:05:49.950
I haven't done anything special.

119
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:50.980
You know what, Renee?

120
00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.420
I don't think any of us are gonna even come close

121
00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.690
to measuring up to what you do.

122
00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.690
So I mean, you're a teacher for a reason.

123
00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.183
'Cause you're teaching a lot more than your students.

124
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.673
Thank you guys so much.

125
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.170
I mean, do you really think that what you do

126
00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.503
is like the bare minimum and not special?

127
00:06:12.488 --> 00:06:14.000
That's what you're supposed to do.

128
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.683
This is what you're supposed to do.

129
00:06:16.893 --> 00:06:19.410
I was always taught, this is what you supposed to do.

130
00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.600
Who taught you that, may I ask?

131
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.453
My mom and my aunt and my grandmother.

132
00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:28.220
They always said, this is what you're supposed to do.

133
00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.070
I just want everyone to be like you.

134
00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.830
And you are our teacher, Renee.

135
00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.720
I can't thank you enough.

136
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:34.553
Thank you.
(audience cheers)

137
00:06:34.553 --> 00:06:36.420
And your mom and your aunt,

138
00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.260
and all your students and their family.

139
00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.810
We just send so much love and gratitude to everyone.

140
00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:45.130
And thank you to BP and Kidpik.

141
00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.842
Thank you so much to these companies.

142
00:06:46.842 --> 00:06:48.370
Thank you.
Thank you.

143
00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.348
And Renee, we'll be in touch.

144
00:06:50.348 --> 00:06:53.015 line:15%
(upbeat music)

